    slug: "ses-aes-qes-which-signature-level-uk",
    title: "SES, AES or QES? Choosing the right UK eIDAS signature level",
    excerpt: "Not every contract needs a qualified signature, and most do not need a wet-ink stamp either. We explain when simple, advanced and qualified electronic signatures fit real UK business workflows, with plain-English examples.",
    category: "UK Law",
    date: "Mar 04, 2026",
    readTime: "8 min read",
    author: "CivicSign Editorial",
    image: "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1589829545856-d10d557cf95f?w=1200&q=80",
    body: [
      { type: "p", content: "UK eIDAS recognises three tiers of electronic signature. Vendors love to sell the highest tier; lawyers love to warn about the lowest. The practical question for most British businesses is simpler: what evidence do you need if someone later denies they signed, or claims the document was altered? That answer maps cleanly onto SES, AES and QES, once you strip away the jargon." },
      { type: "h2", content: "Simple Electronic Signature (SES)" },
      { type: "p", content: "A simple electronic signature is any data attached to or logically associated with a document that the signer uses to sign. Under UK eIDAS Article 3(11), that includes typing your name, drawing on a touchscreen, uploading a signature image, or clicking an clearly labelled 'I agree' button. SES is legally valid for the overwhelming majority of UK commercial contracts." },
      { type: "ul", content: [
        "Best for: NDAs, sales quotes, order forms, consultancy agreements, tenancy paperwork, internal policy acknowledgements, and routine supplier terms.",
        "Evidence you need: signer identity (typically verified email), timestamps, IP address, and a record showing the document viewed before signing.",
        "CivicSign: SES is selectable on Pro when sending, with a full audit trail and Certificate of Completion on every envelope.",
      ]},
      { type: "h2", content: "Advanced Electronic Signature (AES)" },
      { type: "p", content: "An advanced electronic signature must be uniquely linked to the signer, capable of identifying them, created under their sole control, and linked to the data in a way that detects subsequent changes. UK eIDAS Article 26 sets the standard. AES is not magic, it is SES plus stronger identity binding and tamper detection built into the process." },
      { type: "ul", content: [
        "Best for: employment contracts, settlement agreements, high-value B2B contracts, regulated-industry paperwork, and any agreement where you expect a serious dispute.",
        "Extra assurance: stronger signer attribution, platform-level tamper detection, and optional SMS or knowledge-based authentication on Business plans.",
        "CivicSign: Pro users can select AES on Review & Send; Business defaults to AES with optional recipient authentication.",
      ]},
      { type: "callout", content: "Choosing AES is not an admission that SES is weak. It is a risk decision. If the cost of a dispute exceeds the cost of stronger evidence, AES is the sensible default, not because a judge demanded it upfront, but because your file needs to speak for itself." },
      { type: "h2", content: "Qualified Electronic Signature (QES)" },
      { type: "p", content: "A qualified electronic signature is an advanced signature backed by a qualified certificate issued by a Qualified Trust Service Provider (QTSP) listed on the UK trust list. QES carries a statutory presumption of validity equivalent to a handwritten signature in certain cross-border contexts. In domestic UK practice, QES is the exception, not the rule." },
      { type: "ul", content: [
        "Consider QES for: specific EU cross-border transactions, some public-sector tenders, and niche regulated workflows where a QTSP-backed certificate is contractually required.",
        "Usually unnecessary for: standard UK employment, lettings, NDAs, SaaS agreements, and SME supplier contracts.",
        "CivicSign: QES is available on request for Business customers via a QTSP partner, contact us if your counterparty or regulator mandates it.",
      ]},
      { type: "h2", content: "A decision table you can actually use" },
      { type: "ul", content: [
        "Low risk, high volume → SES with a solid audit trail.",
        "HR, legal, or financial exposure → AES.",
        "Counterparty or regulation names a QTSP → QES.",
        "Deed with HM Land Registry → follow Practice Guide 82 (platform process + conveyancer certificate), not generic SES/AES labels alone.",
      ]},
      { type: "h2", content: "What tribunals and courts actually scrutinise" },
      { type: "p", content: "UK courts rarely ask which eIDAS tier you picked. They ask whether the signer intended to be bound, whether they had access to the final text, and whether the document integrity can be demonstrated. A meticulous SES audit trail beats a sloppy AES process every time. Tier choice matters, but process quality matters more." },
      { type: "p", content: "Document your signing policy internally: which agreement types use SES, which default to AES, and who approves exceptions. When CivicSign appends a Certificate of Completion with timestamps, IP data and a SHA-256 seal, you are giving a future judge exactly the narrative they need, regardless of tier." },
    ],
